<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- CONVENTIONS.md "Project reference graph": Contracts references nothing — never Domain,
       never BuildingBlocks. This is Reservation's entire public wire surface: the commands it
       accepts and the events it publishes (ARCHITECTURE.md "Contracts: each service owns and
       publishes its own").

       GL-36 is the first real content: ReserveGift (request/reply, mirrors Identity's
       Login/SignUp) and its ReserveGiftReply, plus the GiftReservedV1 integration event. Purely
       additive over the empty 0.1.0 GL-25 shipped, per the giftlist-contract-change procedure —
       a new type is a minor bump, never a reason to edit anything already here.

       ARCHITECTURE.md "Reservation privacy" is a product guarantee enforced by the ABSENCE of
       fields — no name, no email, no session id, no IP, nothing that could correlate two
       reservations to one person, and no releaseSecret on GiftReservedV1 either (that value
       travels ONLY in ReserveGiftReply). A field added here cannot be removed again without a
       major bump, so the guarantee is cheapest to keep by never adding one. GL-36 also mints the
       one exception on the OTHER side of that same rule: releaseSecret itself, added to the
       Reservation aggregate and to ReserveGiftReply specifically because ARCHITECTURE.md "Data
       model" already named it as the one capability a reserving browser needs and nothing else
       should ever see. -->

  <!-- GL-25: this project is the package. Packed to the local folder feed and consumed by every
       other repo that sends Reservation a command or subscribes to its events. PackageId is the
       assembly name character for character, deliberately: ARCHITECTURE.md "What 'breaking'
       means for a message contract" makes the type name and namespace part of the wire format,
       because Rebus routes and deserializes on the .NET type name.

       0.2.0: GL-36 adds three new types (ReserveGift, ReserveGiftReply, GiftReservedV1) and
       removes nothing — additive, so a minor bump per the giftlist-contract-change procedure,
       never a reused version (NuGet caches by (id, version); repacking 0.1.0 with different
       content would leave any consumer that already restored it silently stale). GL-27 owns the
       bump discipline from here. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Reservations.Contracts</PackageId>
    <Version>0.2.0</Version>
    <Authors>goodsell-engineering</Authors>
    <Description>Reservation's entire public wire surface: the commands it accepts and the events it publishes.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
